A||||9||Hartz||Best ways to clean carpet?||||5||
Z||000000||Hartz||05-14-2001||11:45 AM||||Has anyone tried to steam clean their carpet?  I've tried general carpet cleaners and brushes but the carpet still isn't coming clean.  Before giving up and just replacing it, I was thinking of removing it and using one of those steam cleaners you can rent at the grocery store.  <P>Anyone tried it?<P>Any help appreciated.  Thanks!<P>Hartz<BR>'84 SC 5.7L V8<BR>'85 SE 3.1L V6 Turbo<P>||||reg||5
Z||000001||TWEETIE BIRD||05-14-2001||11:57 AM||||I took mine entirely out, treated the stains and then used the power washer. Did a great job. I also did the trunk compartment and the floormats. <P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.ascenture.net/users/bhhum/fiero1.jpg"> ||||reg||10
Z||000002||skitime||05-14-2001||11:58 AM||||I have had great success cleaning Fiero carpets with our carpet cleaning machine can't remember the brand. This is similar to the rented type.  It is amazing how much dirt, grime, gravel, and spilled coke and coffee gets sucked up.  When you dump the container it is pretty gross.  The machine has a upholster attachment and I use that on the carpet.  It works great on the seats too.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Fiero5||05-14-2001||12:01 PM||||I use "Simple Green" cleaner from the grocery store. Works awesome, either for small areas or a whole section!<P>Eric Schneck says he uses the new Armour All carpet cleaner and says that is awesome also.<P>Steve<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fiero1_5.jpg">  <BR>http://www.kelleyfamilyfieros.homestead.com/index.html||||reg||14
Z||000004||DXR_DAD||05-14-2001||12:13 PM||||To save the trouble of doing it yourself,<BR>renting a machine, trying different cleaners,<BR>removing & reinstalling carpet. I paid the local detail shop $35.00 My carpet looks NEW!<BR>I had more money than time  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> and $35.00 was a good deal for what they did. pre spot, steam clean, and then use some kind of protecting treatment to keep it clean.<P>Just a thought <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>DXR_DAD||||reg||3
Z||000005||Spektyr||05-14-2001||01:37 PM||||Yep, if you taking to a professional shop to get cleaned, they use a REAL steam cleaner.<P>The kind you rent don't actually make steam, just really hot water.  Steam cleaners are huge.  You know the vans that pull up at a house, run a bunch of flexible tubing inside and sound like they're running a industrial size cappucino machine in the back?  Actual steam cleaners.  The kind you rent and drag around the house are just really hot wet-vacs.  If when the time comes to clean my interior (the rest of the car would look wierd if the inside were pristine) it is really hard to clean the carpet, I'm just going to take it to a professional car wash place and have them do it.  I won't let them touch the body though.  After my time in the Navy, I don't trust anyone to wax my car but me.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Fiero5||05-14-2001||01:46 PM||||Did the $35. bucks include all the carpet, even the trunk?<P>Steve<P>------------------<BR>          <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fiero5_3.jpg">        <BR>http://www.kelleyfamilyfieros.homestead.com/index.html||||reg||5
Z||000007||Ken Wittlief||05-14-2001||01:49 PM||||I use a bucket of hot water with liquid laundry soap, Comet cleanser, and a scrub brush, and the garden hose on lightly to rinse it all out<P>  and the wet/dry shop vac to suck the water out of the car. Works pretty well for most dirty carpets. You can pretty mcuh hold the hose nozzel in one hand and the vac nossle in the other, and keep the puddle size to a minimum.<P> I do have a light tan stain where I rest my left foot that I cant seem to get rid of with anything. Was thinking of trying one of the carpet stain removers.<P>  the wet shop vac will suck up just about anything, except gasoline or other flammible liquids - so dont be tempted to try a little gas or paint thinner on the stains, it might be the last thing you do.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Wolfhound||05-14-2001||01:50 PM||||I pull them out and take them to the car wash. They have clamps on the wall to hold floor mats. They work well for the whole carpet half. It,s amazing how much dirt and clay a fairly clean looking carpet will hold.<BR>Use the high pressure soap and high pressure rinse.<p>[This message has been edited by Wolfhound (edited 05-14-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||DXR_DAD||05-14-2001||02:17 PM||||The $35.00 was everything but the trunk!<BR>That I took to the u-spray-it car wash hit it with some spot cleaner let is soak in then hit it with the presure wand, let it sit outside to dry. It was easy to remove and install <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P><BR>DXR_DAD||||reg||14
